Every exact root and exact accumulator lies between min(x,1) and max(x,1): its exponent is in (0,1], since every prefix p<=m<=2^k. Hence exact intermediates are normal and at most two. Outward rounded bounds need not remain in that range and can be subnormal, zero or too wide for finite arithmetic. The absolute rounding argument above, not the original normal-operand guard or the normality of exact intermediates, covers that case. A non-finite upper bound yields no complete numerical reading and does not disprove the exact law. A subnormal or zero lower bound is retained, not reclassified as a below-normal original operand.

